{{Strong|''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum''}} (formerly ''Aster lanceolatus'' and ''Aster simplex'') is a species of flowering plant in the [[Family (botany)|family]] [[Asteraceae]] [[Native plant|native]] to North America. [[Common name]]s include {{Strong|panicled aster}}, {{Strong|lance-leaved aster}}, and {{Strong|white panicled aster}}. It is a [[Perennial plant|perennial]], [[herbaceous plant]] that may reach {{convert|1.5|m|ft|abbr=off|sp=us|frac=2}} tall or more, sometimes approaching {{convert|2|m|ft|abbr=on|sp=us|frac=2}}. The lance-shaped leaves are generally hairless but may feel slightly rough to the touch on the top because of tiny bristles. ''S.&nbsp;lanceolatum'' blooms July to October. The flowers grow in clusters and branch in [[panicle]]s. They have 16–50 white [[ray floret]]s that are up to {{convert|14|mm|in|abbr=off|sp=us|frac=8}} long and sometimes tinged pink or purple. The flower centers consist of [[disk floret]]s that begin as yellow and become purple as they mature.

The species occurs in a wide variety of mostly moist and open habitats, including [[riparian areas]] (areas between land and a river or stream), [[meadows]], and [[ditches]]. Panicled aster has a stout [[rhizome]] and can spread to form a [[clonal colony]] as well as reproduce by wind-blown seed. Because of its rhizomatic spreading and its [[Allelopathy|production of chemicals]] that can be detrimental to other plants around it, the species can do damage to [[ecosystem]]s outside of its [[native range]], as it has in Europe where it has been [[Introduced species|introduced]].

''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' is currently divided into [[subspecies]] and [[Variety (botany)|varieties]] which have minor differences in appearance and vary in chromosome counts as well as [[Species distribution|distribution]], with some overlap. It is a conservationally secure species whose late-summer and fall appearing flowers play an important role for late-season pollinators and nectar-seeking insects such as bumblebees, wasps, and hoverflies. In addition to being used by [[indigenous peoples of the Americas]] for medicinal purposes, it has been [[Gardening|cultivated]] as an [[ornamental garden plant]] and used in the [[cut flower]] industry.

==Description==
<!--Symphyotrichum lanceolatum 93578204.jpg-->
Panicled aster grows from a [[rhizome]] and has a thick, erect stem that can reach {{convert|1.5|m|ft|abbr=off|sp=us|frac=2}} tall or more, sometimes approaching {{convert|2|m|ft|abbr=on|sp=us|frac=2}}. The leaves are generally hairless but may feel slightly rough to the touch on the top because of tiny bristles. The leaf blades have winged [[Petiole (botany)|petioles]] and may sheath the stem at their bases. The largest leaves, near the base of the plant, are up to about {{convert|15|cm|in|0|abbr=off|sp=us}} long. Those higher on the stem are smaller, and the lower leaves may have toothed edges.{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020a}}

''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' blooms July to October. The [[inflorescence]] is usually a large, branching [[panicle]]d array of many [[Pseudanthium|flower heads]] of varying size. Each flower head has many tiny [[florets]] put together into what appear as one. There are 16 to 50 [[ray florets]] per head, each measuring {{convert|3-14|mm|in|abbr=off|sp=us|frac=8}} long, in white, sometimes tinged pink or purple. The roughly 20–40 [[disk floret]]s bloom yellow and turn purple. Each has five lobes that may spread when open.{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020a}}

===Chromosomes===
''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' has a base number of {{Nowrap|'''''x''''' {{=}} 8.{{sfn|Semple|n.d.}}}} [[Tetraploid]], [[pentaploid]], [[hexaploid]], [[septaploid]], and [[octaploid]] cytotypes with respective [[chromosome]] counts of 32, 40, 48, 56, and 64 have been reported, depending on the [[Infraspecific name|infraspecies]], as follows:

* ''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' var. ''hesperium'': '''''2n''''' = 8''x'' = 64.{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020b}}
* ''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' var. ''hirsuticaule'': '''''2n''''' = 4''x'' = 32.{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020c}}
* ''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' var. ''interior'': '''''2n''''' = 6''x'' = 48 and '''''2n''''' = 8''x'' = 64.{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020d}}
* ''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' var. ''lanceolatum'': '''''2n''''' = 4''x'' = 32, '''''2n''''' = 5''x'' = 40, '''''2n''''' = 6''x'' = 48, '''''2n''''' = 7''x'' = 56, and '''''2n''''' = 8''x'' = 64.{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020e}}
* ''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' var. ''latifolium'': '''''2n''''' = 8''x'' = 64.{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020f}}

==Taxonomy==
[[File:Western stream aster, Symphyotrichum lanceolatum var. hesperium (16091146409).jpg|thumb|alt=|{{Nowrap|''S. lanceolatum'' var.}} ''hesperium'' [[White Mountains (California)|White Mountains, Nevada]]]]
''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' was formerly included in the large genus ''Aster'' as ''Aster lanceolatus''. However, this broad circumscription of ''Aster'' is [[polyphyletic]] and the North American asters are now mostly classified in ''[[Symphyotrichum]]'' and several [[List of Aster synonyms|other genera]].{{sfn|Semple|2021a}} It is classified in the [[subgenus]] ''Symphyotrichum'', [[Section (botany)|section]] ''Symphyotrichum'', [[Section (botany)|subsection]] ''Dumosi'',{{sfn|Semple|2021b}} one of the "bushy asters and relatives".{{sfn|Semple|n.d.}}

