===Hybrids===
[[File:Symphyotrichum x salignum 48675557 iNat.jpg|thumb|''Symphyotrichum'' × ''salignum'' growing in [[Lobnya]], [[Moscow Oblast]], [[Russia]]]]
The [[Hybrid (biology)|hybrid]] of {{Nowrap|''S. lanceolatum'' var.}} ''lanceolatum'' with [[Symphyotrichum novi-belgii|{{Nowrap|''S. novi-belgii'' var.}} ''novi-begii'']], known as {{Nowrap|''Sympyotrichum'' ×}} ''salignum'', originated in [[Gardening|cultivation]] and is now naturalized in Europe. Hybrids also have been reported with {{Nowrap|''[[Symphyotrichum boreale|S. boreale]]''}}, {{Nowrap|''[[Symphyotrichum laeve|S. laeve]]''}}, {{Nowrap|''[[Symphyotrichum lateriflorum|S. lateriflorum]]''}}, {{Nowrap|''[[Symphyotrichum puniceum|S. puniceum]]''}} and {{Nowrap|''[[Symphyotrichum racemosum|S. racemosum]]''}}.{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020e}}

===Etymology===
''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' is [[Common name|commonly known]] as panicled aster, white panicled aster, and lance-leaved aster.{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020a}} Other common names include tall white aster,{{sfn|Dickinson|Metsger|Bull|Dickinson|2004}} eastern line aster, lance-leaf aster, and white-panicle aster.{{sfn|GRIN|n.d.a}}

Along with other asters that bloom in the fall, ''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' may be called a [[Michaelmas]] daisy. Narrow-leaf Michaelmas daisy is also one of its common names.{{sfn|GRIN|n.d.a}} ''S.&nbsp;lanceolatum'' var. ''interior'' may be called interior white aster,{{sfn|NatureServe|2021e}} and ''S.&nbsp;lanceolatum'' var. ''latifolium'' may be called broadleaf panicled aster.{{sfn|NatureServe|2021f}} ''S.&nbsp;lanceolatum'' var. ''hesperium'' common names include western line aster, western willow aster, Wooten's aster, and Siskiyou aster.{{sfn|GRIN|n.d.b}} Another species in the family Asteraceae, ''[[Eucephalus glabratus]]'', is also commonly known as Siskiyou aster.{{sfn|CNPS Rare Plant Program|2021}}

''Aster'' comes from the [[Ancient Greek]] word {{lang|grc|[[:wikt:ἀστήρ|ἀστήρ]]}} ({{Lang|grc-Latn|astḗr}}), meaning "star", referring to the shape of the flower. The word "aster" was used to describe a star-like flower as early as 1542 in German physician and botanist [[Leonhart Fuchs]]' book {{lang|la|[[De historia stirpium commentarii insignes]]}}, Latin for ''Notable Commentaries on the History of Plants''. An old common name for [[Astereae]] species using the suffix "[[Wort (plant)|-wort]]" is starwort, also spelled star-wort or star wort. An early use of this name can be found in the same work by Fuchs as {{lang|de|Sternkraut}}, translated from German literally as "star herb" ({{wikt-lang|de|Stern}} {{wikt-lang|de|Kraut}}).{{sfn|Fuchs|1542}}

The [[Specific epithet (botany)|specific epithet]] (second part of the [[Binomial nomenclature|binomial name]]) ''[[:wikt:lanceolatum|lanceolatum]]'' is a [[Latin]] adjective meaning "lance-shaped".{{sfn|Wilhelm|Rericha|2017|p=1101}} The genus name ''[[Symphyotrichum]]'' is a combining of Ancient Greek words meaning "junction of hair".{{sfn|Wilhelm|Rericha|2017|p=1095}}

==Distribution and habitat==
===Native===
''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' is [[Native plant|native]] to much of Canada, the United States, and northwestern Mexico.{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020a}}{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020b}} In its [[native range]], it occurs in a wide variety of mostly moist and open habitats, including [[riparian area]]s, [[meadow]]s, and [[ditch]]es. Distribution and habitat vary among the infraspecies.{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020e}}{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020b}} 

{{Nowrap|''S. lanceolatum'' var. ''lanceolatum''}} is widespread in central and northeastern North America. It is absent in the west and southeast. It grows at {{convert|0–900|m|ft|-1|abbr=off|sp=us}} in stream banks, [[thicket]] borders, meadows, fields, and ditches.{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020e}}

{{Nowrap|''S. lanceolatum'' var. ''hesperium''}} is a widespread western species native to North America in the [[Northwest Territories]], from [[British Columbia]] to [[Quebec]]; nearly all the [[contiguous United States]] west of the [[Mississippi River]], plus [[Wisconsin]]; as well as the Mexican states of [[Baja California]], [[Sonora]] and [[Chihuahua (state)|Chihuahua]]. It grows at {{convert|10–2700|m|ft|-1|abbr=on}} on the edges of [[stream]]s in [[prairie]]s, [[wet meadow]]s, [[Montane ecosystems|open slopes]] of [[Temperate coniferous forest|mountainous pine forests]], roadside ditches, and can live in [[calcareous soil]].{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020b}}

{{Nowrap|''S. lanceolatum'' var.}} ''hirsuticaule'' is native to the northwestern [[Great Lakes region]] as well as southeast [[Manitoba]] in "[[Muck (soil)|mucky soils]] on [[glacial deposit]]s", and it can be found at {{convert|100–300|m|ft|-1|abbr=on}}.{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020c}}

{{Nowrap|''S. lanceolatum'' var.}} ''interior'' is native to streams in [[lowland]]s at {{convert|10–400|m|ft|-1|abbr=on}} in most of the Great Lakes region of North America as well as the central United States.{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020d}}

{{Nowrap|''S. lanceolatum'' var.}} ''latifolium'' is a very widespread central and eastern species where grows in thickets, [[deciduous wood]]s borders, [[stream bank]]s, and ditches at {{convert|0–800|m|ft|-1|abbr=on}}. It is the only variety native to the southeastern United States.{{sfn|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2020f}}

