===Introduced===
''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' has been [[Introduced species|introduced]] and [[Naturalisation (biology)|naturalized]] in many parts of Europe, from [[Belgium]] to [[Serbia]] to [[Latvia]], where it occurs in disturbed man-made habitats and riparian areas as an [[invasive species]].{{sfn|Nešić|Obratov-Petković|Skočajić|Bjedov|2016}}{{sfn|Gudžinskas|Petrulaitis|2016}}{{sfn|Verloove|2018}} {{As of|2020}}, it was not on the [[European Union|European Union's]] [[List of invasive alien species of Union concern]].{{sfn|European Commission|2020}}
[[File:Symphyotrichum lanceolatum july 6 2019.jpg|thumb|right|Large clone of ''S.&nbsp;lanceolatum'' prior to flowering, Ontario, Canada]]

==Ecology==
[[File:Symphyotrichum lanceolatum - Lance Leaf Aster.jpg|thumb|Bumblebee on ''S.&nbsp;lanceolatum'', Fairfax County, Virginia]]
In its native range, ''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' may be a minor [[weed]] in agricultural fields.{{sfn|Chmielewski|Semple|2001|p=838}} In addition to [[seed dispersal|dispersal]] by wind-blown seed, it spreads extensively by rhizomes to create large [[Clonal colony|clonal colonies]].{{sfn|Chmielewski|Semple|2001|p=843}} Although these clones do not dominate habitats in North America, growing in association with [[grasses]], [[goldenrods]], and other asters,{{sfn|Chmielewski|Semple|2001|p=840}} in Europe it is an invasive species that excludes native plants in riparian habitats. This invasiveness has been linked to [[allelopathic]] compounds in {{Nowrap|''S. lanceolatum''}} tissue and their effects on native European plants.{{sfn|Nešić|Obratov-Petković|Skočajić|Bjedov|2016}} Viable seed production in Europe seems to be limited, and the species largely spreads there via rhizomes.{{sfn|Verloove|2018}}
[[File:Haltern am See, Westruper Heide -- 2016 -- 5987.jpg|thumb|right|Fruiting ''S.&nbsp;lanceolatum'', Germany]]

The species is visited by a wide variety of late-season [[Pollinator|pollinating]] and nectar-seeking insects, including bees, hoverflies, flies, moths, and wasps.{{sfn|Wilhelm|Rericha|2017|p=1101}} [[Cross-fertilization]] is usually required to produce viable seed, and as such, large clones may have low seed production.{{sfn|Chmielewski|Semple|2001|p=842}}

===Pests and diseases===
Several midge species are known to form [[galls]] on ''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' where their larvae can develop, including ''[[Rhopalomyia asteriflorae]]'' in the flowers or buds resulting in their stunted growth,{{sfn|Chmielewski|Semple|2001|p=846}} and ''[[Rhopalomyia strobiligemma]]''.{{sfn|Wilhelm|Rericha|2017|p=1101}} 

The leaf-blister gall midge ''[[Asteromyia paniculata]]'' and the fungus ''[[Schlerotium asteris]]'' have a symbiotic relationship on the leaves in that the fungus gains additional nutrition from the larva and, in turn, gives some shelter to the midge.{{sfn|Chmielewski|Semple|2001|p=846}}

Leaf-mining insects known to feed on this species include ''[[Sumitrosis inaequalis]]'', ''[[Ophiomyia curvipalpis]]''<!--http://diptera.org/Nomenclator/Details/140578 shows this as the accepted name of Agromyza curvipalpis which may explain why Agromyza curvipalpis cannot be found!-->, ''[[Phytomyza albiceps]]'', and ''[[Microrhopala xerene]]''. Younger [[instar]]s of gorgone checkerspot caterpillars (''[[Chlosyne gorgone]]'') will feed on {{Nowrap|''S. lanceolatum''}}.{{sfn|Chmielewski|Semple|2001|p=846}}

==Conservation==
[[NatureServe]] lists the species ''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' as a whole as [[NatureServe conservation status|Secure (G5)]] worldwide; Vulnerable (S3) in Iowa; and, Imperiled (S2) in North Carolina and Newfoundland.{{sfn|NatureServe|2021a}} {{Nowrap|''S. l.'' subsp.}} ''hesperium'', {{Nowrap|''S. l.'' subsp.}} ''lanceolatum'', {{Nowrap|''S. l.'' var.}} ''interior'', and {{Nowrap|''S. l.'' var.}} ''latifolium'' are all listed as a Secure Subspecies (T5) globally.{{sfn|NatureServe|2021b}}{{sfn|NatureServe|2021c}}{{sfn|NatureServe|2021e}}{{sfn|NatureServe|2021f}} NatureServe has no global ranking for {{Nowrap|''S. l.'' var.}} ''hirsuticaule''.{{sfn|NatureServe|2021d}}

In individual states and provinces of the United States and Canada, {{Nowrap|''S. lanceolatum'' subsp.}} ''hesperium'' is Vulnerable (S3) in Wyoming, and Critically Imperiled (S1) in Texas;{{sfn|NatureServe|2021b}} {{Nowrap|''S. l.'' var.}} ''interior'' is Possibly Extirpated (SH) in New York state and Quebec, and Critically Imperiled (S1) in New Jersey;{{sfn|NatureServe|2021e}} and, {{Nowrap|''S. l.'' var.}} ''latifolium'' is Vulnerable (S3) in Ontario and Georgia.{{sfn|NatureServe|2021f}} {{Nowrap|''S. l.'' var.}} ''hirsuticaule'' has no vulnerable or critical state or province rankings.{{sfn|NatureServe|2021d}}

==Uses==
===Medicinal===
Within its native range, ''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' has been used by [[Indigenous peoples of the Americas|indigenous peoples]] for a variety of medicinal purposes: {{Nowrap|''S. l.'' var.}} ''hesperium'' by the [[Zuni people]] in the [[American Southwest]] for wounds and nosebleed,{{sfn|Stevenson|1915}} and {{Nowrap|''S. l.'' var.}} ''lanceolatum'' by the [[Iroquois]] in what is now [[eastern Canada]] to treat [[fever]].{{sfn|Rousseau|1945}}

===Gardening===
''Symphyotrichum lanceolatum'' has been cultivated as an [[ornamental garden plant]] and used in the [[cut flower]] industry.{{sfn|Chmielewski|Semple|2001|p=839}} [[Cultivar]] 'Edwin Beckett', developed before 1902, has pale violet-blue ray florets that make a flower head which is about {{convert|25|mm|in|abbr=off|sp=us|frac=8}} wide.{{sfn|Picton|1999|p=132}} {{As of|2021|July}}, it was listed in the [[Royal Horticultural Society]] Plant Finder with availability at 3–4 nurseries.{{sfn|Royal Horticultural Society|n.d.}}
